# Optional advanced overrides. You do NOT need a .env file: the setup wizard (open Kipple in a
# browser after the first start and paste the setup code from `docker logs kipple`) creates the
# account and sets the time zone, theme and feeds, and Settings covers the rest. Copy this to .env
# only for something below. Every line is commented out, so an untouched copy changes nothing;
# remove the leading "# " from a line to use it. Never commit .env; only this file is checked in.
# See internal/config/config.go for parsing and defaults.
#
# Where a variable and the app both cover the same thing (KIPPLE_ADDR, TZ), the variable wins, so
# scripted deploys stay reproducible. The account variables are the exception: they only create the
# account on a first start and never change one that exists.

# --- Server ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Address the HTTP server listens on. Default ":1919" (all interfaces inside the container; the
# compose files publish it on 127.0.0.1 only). If the address is taken, Kipple exits with an
# error naming it and this variable.
#
# Existing installs: since 0.6.0 an unset value always means ":1919" (there is no longer a fallback
# to the pre-0.5 ":7080"). To stay on 7080, set KIPPLE_ADDR=:7080 (and keep a "7080:7080" port
# mapping); to move, set :1919 and change the mapping. Setting it always wins.
# Change the port mapping in your compose file together with this value.
# KIPPLE_ADDR=:1919

# Directory holding the SQLite database and other persistent state. Default "/data" (the image
# creates it owned by the nonroot user; the compose files mount a named volume there).
# KIPPLE_DATA=/data

# --- Account (optional: the setup wizard does this) ------------------------

# Create the single account from the environment instead of the browser wizard, for scripted
# deploys. Both must be set: username 1-64 characters of A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ -, password 5 to 256
# characters (the old example value "change-me" is refused). Used only on the first start with an
# empty database; an account created this way never sees the wizard's onboarding steps. Changing
# them later has no effect: use `kipple password` to reset the password, and remove the lines
# afterwards. With either one missing (a lone KIPPLE_USERNAME is ignored) Kipple starts in setup
# mode and the wizard asks, and an empty KIPPLE_PASSWORD never means "no password".
# KIPPLE_USERNAME=owner
# KIPPLE_PASSWORD=

# Initial Google Reader API (sync client) password, 16 to 256 characters (it guards the public sync
# endpoint). If unset, generate one in the wizard's last step or Settings, or run
# `kipple api-password` (recommended: it makes a random 24-character one). Applied at start only
# while the account has no Reader API password; it never replaces an existing one. A value outside
# that range stops the start that would apply it to a new account.
# KIPPLE_API_PASSWORD=

# --- Cloudflare Access (optional) ----------------------------------------

# Leave both unset unless Cloudflare Access sits in front of Kipple. Kipple never needs Cloudflare;
# with both unset this feature is off. Set both or neither: one without the other stops startup.
#
# When set, Kipple checks the Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion header Access adds to each request it lets
# through: the RS256 signature against the team's keys at
# https://<team domain>/cdn-cgi/access/certs (cached for an hour), the issuer
# (https://<team domain>), the audience (the AUD below) and the expiry. A verified token never
# replaces the session cookie. It does two things:
# - Settings shows the Access email you are signed in with.
# - The web password becomes optional: the wizard offers "No password, through Cloudflare Access" when
#   you open it through Access, and Settings > Remove web password does the same later (only offered
#   through Access, and it asks for the current password). An account without a password signs in
#   ONLY on requests carrying a verified token, so going around Access (the LAN, a published port)
#   cannot sign in. Anyone your Access policy for this application admits can: keep it to your own
#   email. If you later unset these variables, set a password again with `kipple password`.
# The Reader API (sync apps) always keeps its own API password.
#
# Team domain: Zero Trust > Settings > Custom Pages, e.g. yourteam.cloudflareaccess.com (https://
# is optional; no path).
# KIPPLE_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=yourteam.cloudflareaccess.com
# Application Audience (AUD) tag: Zero Trust > Access > Applications > your Kipple application >
# Overview.
# KIPPLE_ACCESS_AUD=

# --- Networking ---------------------------------------------------------

# Public URL Kipple is reached at. It is the "+url" part of the fetch User-Agent and the base of
# Reader API iconUrl values (when the greader.icon_urls setting is on). OPML exports do not use it.
# If empty, sync apps get no feed icons. It must be an absolute http(s) URL with a host and no
# query, fragment or user info, or startup stops. Its host is also allowed by the Host check below.
# Example: https://rss.example.com
# KIPPLE_PUBLIC_URL=

# Comma-separated IP addresses and CIDR ranges (192.0.2.10, 198.51.100.0/24, 2001:db8::/32) allowed
# to tell Kipple who the client is: X-Forwarded-For (the rightmost hop that is not itself listed),
# CF-Connecting-IP when there is no X-Forwarded-For, and X-Forwarded-Proto (the Secure cookie
# flag). The client address keys the web sign-in and sync login budgets, which group an IPv6
# client by its /64. Set this to the address(es) your reverse proxy / tunnel connects FROM as
# Kipple sees them, e.g. 192.0.2.10 (a placeholder; use your own), and never a range wider than
# your proxies. Trust a Docker network (or any range clients can reach directly) ONLY when the
# published port is reachable by the proxy alone: otherwise any client in it can write its own
# X-Forwarded-For / CF-Connecting-IP and be believed. A trusted peer also makes open mode refuse
# the request as "forwarded" (it fails closed). Leave unset to always use the TCP peer address.
# If a request carries one of those headers from a peer that is not listed, the header is ignored
# and Kipple logs a WARN (at most once an hour) naming the peer, so a missing entry is easy to
# spot: without it every visitor shares the proxy's address and one budget (wrong passwords are
# slowed, never counted against anyone else; even one persistent guesser on a shared address can make
# sign-in answer "busy, try again", never a lockout).
# KIPPLE_TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=

# Comma-separated extra host names Kipple answers to during first-run setup and while it runs
# without a password ("open mode"), e.g. rss.example.com or *.example.com (no scheme or port).
# This is DNS-rebinding protection: in those two modes a request whose Host header is not allowed
# gets "421 Misdirected Request" naming this setting. Always allowed: IP addresses (so
# http://<ip>:1919 always works), localhost, names ending in .localhost or .ts.net (Tailscale), and
# the host of KIPPLE_PUBLIC_URL. During setup also single-word names such as nas and names ending in
# .local, .lan, .home.arpa or .internal; open mode refuses those unless listed here, because other
# devices on the local network can answer them (mDNS, LLMNR, router DHCP names). With a
# password (the normal case) other names are only logged, never refused. More names can be added
# later in Settings (security.allowed_hosts); this variable exists for setup, before any settings
# screen does.
# KIPPLE_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# --- Time -----------------------------------------------------------------

# IANA time zone, e.g. America/New_York. When set it wins over the time zone chosen in the setup
# wizard or Settings: it is then the zone for everything (log timestamps, daily reading statistics,
# the nightly 04:10 maintenance and backup file names) and Settings shows the in-app time zone
# read-only. When unset, the time zone chosen in the wizard or Settings is used (UTC until chosen
# for a new install; America/New_York for a database created before 0.5), and changing it needs no
# restart. Before 0.5 this variable only set the log timestamps, so an old TZ line left here now
# also moves statistics; Kipple logs a WARN at start when TZ and the in-app zone differ. An unknown
# name stops startup. time/tzdata is compiled in, so this resolves without a filesystem zoneinfo
# database.
# TZ=America/New_York

# --- Fetch scheduler -----------------------------------------------------

# How often the scheduler checks for feeds due to be polled. Default 30s, minimum 1s.
# KIPPLE_SCHED_TICK=30s

# Number of concurrent fetch worker goroutines. Default 8.
# KIPPLE_FETCH_WORKERS=8

# Max concurrent in-flight fetches per host. Default 2.
# KIPPLE_FETCH_PER_HOST=2

# --- Logging ------------------------------------------------------------

# log/slog level: debug, info, warn or error. Default info. (The one-time setup code is printed
# outside the log levels, so "error" cannot hide it.)
# KIPPLE_LOG_LEVEL=info

# Set to 1 to also log Google Reader API form values (truncated; the password and tokens are
# always redacted) at debug level with KIPPLE_LOG_LEVEL=debug. Useful for diagnosing a new sync
# client. Default unset/false.
# KIPPLE_LOG_GREADER_FORMS=

# --- Go runtime -----------------------------------------------------------

# GOMEMLIMIT=64MiB   # set in docker-compose.example.yml, with mem_limit: 256m; a soft
#                    # Go heap limit (SQLite's own heap is outside it)
